CERN, European Organization for Nuclear Research1211 Geneva 23SwitzerlandJ.D. Blakeblake@cernvax.cern.ch1991/2/28                   GNU C Compiler (gcc) for OS-9/68000                   -----------------------------------The GNU C Compiler (gcc) for OS-9/68000 has been prepared for distributionby  anonymous  FTP  from friendly server sites.  Offers of FTP hospitalitywill be  welcomed,  as  CERN  does  not  run  an  anonymous  FTP  service.Executable  and run time library files in the distribution were built on asystem running OS-9/68000 version 2.4 and C version 3.2.  They  have  beentested under OS-9/68000 versions 2.3 and 2.4.There are four distribution files:    gcc-1.37.1-osk-README       Basic information (this file)    gcc-1.37.1-osk-bin.tar.Z    OS-9/68000 executable gcc    gcc-1.37.1-osk-doc.tar.Z    Gcc and cpp user guides    gcc-1.37.1-osk-src.tar.Z    Source code (cross and native gcc)The first file contains ASCII text; the other files are in compressed  tar(binary)  format.  My  working  assumption  is  that  the  recipient  willtransfer each distribution file to a UNIX  system  and  unpack  it  there,using  the  programs  uncompress  and  tar; the unpacked files may then betransferred to the target OS-9/68000 system by FTP.  The approximate  sizeof each distribution file is:    gcc-1.37.1-osk-README       13 Kbytes    gcc-1.37.1-osk-bin.tar.Z    432 Kbytes  (707 Kbytes uncompressed)    gcc-1.37.1-osk-doc.tar.Z    945 Kbytes  (2335 Kbytes uncompressed)    gcc-1.37.1-osk-src.tar.Z    1432 Kbytes (3974 Kbytes uncompressed)The GNU C compiler is free software.  Permission to copy is given  in  thefile COPYING which is included in each distribution file.The executable distribution file includes all files needed to use gcc as anative OS-9/68000 compiler.  There is some practical documentation, but nofull length manuals.The documentation distribution file contains user guides to gcc and to itspreprocessor:    Using and Porting GNU CC    Richard M. Stallman    188 pages    The C Preprocessor    Richard M. Stallman    43 pagesin source  form  (for  TeX)  and  in  printable  forms  (TeX  dvi  format,Postscript).The three distribution files, taken together, should contain all the filesneeded  to  install,  modify  and  use  gcc  as  a  native  compiler on anOS-9/68000 system.  The source code distribution also  contains  files  tobuild  a  cross compiler gccos9, which runs under VAX Ultrix and generatescode for OS-9/68000.Gcc for OS-9/68000 is based on gcc  1.37.1  which  may  be  obtained  fromprep.ai.mit.edu  by  anonymous  FTP;  look  in the directory pub/gnu.  Thestandard distribution of gcc 1.37.1 contains many  files  which  have  notbeen repeated in the OS-9/68000 distribution, in order to save space.Gcc for OS-9/68000 runs at CERN as a native compiler on OS-9/68000 systemsand  as  a  cross  compiler  on  cernvax.  It  can  compile  code  for the68000/68010 and for the 68020/68030 with or without  68881/68882  floatingpoint  coprocessor.  The compiler uses Microware's assembly code optimizer(o68), assemblers (r68, r68020), linker (l68), standard header  files  andrun time libraries.The principal author of gcc is Richard Stallman of MIT.  Gcc  was  adaptedto OS-9/68000 by Atsushi Seyama of Tokyo.  Further modifications have beenmade by Martin Husemann and Kei Thomsen of Paderborn  University,  and  byJulian Blake of CERN.  Fuller lists of contributors to gcc may be found in"Using and Porting GNU CC", and in  the  files  Doc  and  History  in  theOS-9/68000 gcc distribution.Contents of gcc-1.37.1-osk-bin.tar.Z------------------------------------r--r--r--605/20   12488 Feb  6 17:09 1991 COPYINGr--r--r--605/20    3703 Feb 18 09:32 1991 Commentsr--r--r--605/20   26738 Feb  6 17:09 1991 Docr--r--r--605/20    6505 Feb 28 12:47 1991 Historyr--r--r--605/20   11995 Feb 28 12:47 1991 READMEr--r--r--605/20     858 Feb  6 17:09 1991 assert